<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakarta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jayakarta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakarta is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Its port, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa">Sunda Kelapa</a>, was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatahillah">Fatahillah</a> in 1527 and renamed Jayakarta. In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> seized it and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, its Asian headquarters and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. The city took the name Jakarta in 1942 and became the capital of independent Indonesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakarta.jpg" alt="Jakarta" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Medelam · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundaran_Hotel_Indonesia_(2025)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Japanese occupation of 1942–1945 destroyed Dutch authority, and on 17 August 1945, two days after Japan's surrender, Sukarno and Mohammad Hatta proclaimed independence in <b>Jakarta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The stone pillar set up for Sunda’s 1522 treaty with Portugal was rediscovered in <b>Jakarta</b> in 1918 and is now kept in the National Museum of Indonesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centres include the Centre for South-South Technical Cooperation in <b>Jakarta</b>, set up after the Cartagena meeting of 1995, and others in Cuba, Iran and India.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Living in <b>Jakarta</b>, he read an essay in Timbul magazine that challenged Indonesian musicians to compose a future national anthem, and he took up the challenge, finishing a song called &quot;Indonesia&quot; in Bandung in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lokananta record company gave it, on a souvenir LP of Indonesian folk songs, to the participants of the 4th Asian Games in <b>Jakarta</b> in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1871 King Chulalongkorn visited Singapore and Batavia (now <b>Jakarta</b>) and found that Siam was using the same anthem as Great Britain, which then ruled Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li></ul></section>
